#Healthcare Claims Data - A Field Guide

A primer on how healthcare claims data is actually shaped.

---

## The Core Domains

A claims dataset almost always arrives as a set of related domains:

Domain | Grain (one row per) | Role

Claims | claim line (detail) or claim (header) | The financial + clinical event; what was done, billed, allowed, paid
Member | member x coverage period | Who recieved care (eligibility, demographics, enrollment)
Provider | provider (location/network) | Who delivered care (NPI, TIN, specialty, network status)
Group / Plan | employer group or benefit plan | The coverage context (line of business, product)
COB | member x other coverage | Coordination of Benefits (other payers)
Pharmacy | Rx claim line | Drug claims

The claims domain is the fact table; the rest are reference/lookup tables you join to it. Almost every row-explosion bug lives in one of these joins.

## Header vs. detail; the grain that trips everyone up

A claim has two natural levels:

Header - one row per claim. Carries claim-level facts and sums of the lines (total billed, total allowed, total paid).
Detail / line - one row per service line. Carries per-line values (this procedure, revenue code, this line's paid amount).

The pairing rule: most financial fields exist at both levels. A header amount is the SUM of its detail line, repeated on every line at header grain. When you touch a financial field check
for its partner.

SUM(detail.amount) per claim == header.amount

Two classic defects:
-header popualted with a single line's value instead of the sum --> understated totals.
-A field that should be per-line is rpeeated at header grain --> double-counting when re-aggregated downstream.

Grain alos matters across layers. A source/raw layer may be line grain while a consumer model is header grain with the lines nested in an array. You cannot validate a collapsed/aggreaged output directly against
raw input - different grain. Cmpare like-to-like or validate the output pattern.

## Claim tpe drives everything

Claims split into three types, and this single attribute governs which fields should be populated. 

Type | Meaning | Typical Form
I - inpatient | Facility, admited | UB-04 / 837I
O - Outpatient | Facility, not admitted | Ub-04 / 837I
P - Profesional | Physician / Clinician | CMS-1500 / 837P

Key Insight: a field that is populated across all claim types is usually wrong. Revenue codes are institutional only. 
DRGs are inpatient only, procedure codes don't belong on inpatient. See primer 02 for the full applicability matrix. When a field's popluation rate looks too low, set the realistic ceiling
by claim type first before calling it a defect - ie an instutional only conccept will look sparse in a professional heavy population, and that's correct.

## THe Claim lifecycle: versions and adjustments

Claims are not static. A claim is submitted, adjudicated, sometimes reversed, and re-adjusticated. Each pass produceds a version. (an adjustment). The same logical claim can appear many times in a raw feed.
Downstream analytics almost always wants the final action - the single most-recent, authortative version of each claim, with the interim/duplicate versions removed. Getting this right is foundational
getting it wrong silently double-counts money. See primer 05.

Common lifecycle fields.
-claim key /id - uniquer per version
-root claim id - the verison-independent id (the key with version suffix stripped). All versions of one claim share a root.
-Version / adjustment number - increpents per readjudication
-Adjudication date, paid date, received date - used to rank versions
-Payment status - paid, pending, denied, reversed. usually derived from a raw status-code

## Identififers you'll normalize constantly

Member Id (+ dependent number) : the member key is often not unique without the dependent number. Joining on member alone collapses dependents and explodes enrollment. 
Provider ID / NPI / TIN - providers frequently have multiple rows per ID, dedup before joining
Normilization is a two sided conract - if you strip leading zeroes or left pad an ID on one side, do it on both sides.

## Money, units, dates: format conversions

Standadized claims layers usually store

-Amounts as implied decimal integers: never assume a literal decimal string: confirm the fields picture/length agaisnt the layout spec
-units scaled (often x1000)
-Dates as YYYYmmDD, with sentienls meaning missing, not a real date

Format compliance is not value correctness. A value can be numerically right and still violate the required format. Validate both.

## Summary

Claims data is a versioned fact table at two grains whos fields are conditoinal on claim type, joined to a historical reference table that must be deduplicated first. Almost every bug is a 
violation of one of those four truths.
